{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3179","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3179","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3179\",\"parent_revision\":\"83a56f9949274685b3b5e75c7815f48918faa2195598a9356ee49f7d3da4e73b\",\"root_revision\":\"83a56f9949274685b3b5e75c7815f48918faa2195598a9356ee49f7d3da4e73b\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nTwo tests in `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_network_guard.py` fail when the plugin suite runs as a whole but pass when the file runs alone. Reproduced on unmodified `main` as well as on feature branches, so this is not caused by any branch's changes.\\n\\nAffected tests:\\n- `test_no_public_allow_network_fixture`\\n- `test_guard_restores_sockets_after_session`\\n\\nObserved 2026-08-23, from `plugins/development-harness`:\\n- `uv run pytest backlog_core tests -q` \u2192 `1 failed, 3458 passed` (the failure being `test_no_public_allow_network_fixture`)\\n- `uv run pytest tests/test_network_guard.py -q` \u2192 `9 passed`\\n\\nThe name of the second test points at the likely mechanism: the guard patches sockets for the session and restores them afterwards, so the outcome depends on what else in the run has touched sockets and in what order. The suite runs under `pytest-xdist` with `-n auto --dist loadgroup`, so which worker picks up which test varies between runs, which would also explain why only one of the two failed in the run above.\\n\\nImpact if left: every full-suite run produces a failure that has to be manually recognised and dismissed as known. That is corrosive \u2014 during this session it was dismissed on sight several times, and a genuine new failure appearing alongside it could easily be waved through the same way. An order-dependent test is a real defect in the test, not environmental noise.\\n\\nA third pre-existing failure was recorded in the same session and may or may not share a cause: `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_github_sync_provider.py::test_github_content_provider_replay_preserves_concurrent_remote_revision`. It was never investigated. Confirm whether it is also order-dependent or a separate issue during grooming.\\n\\nUser-provided context: `@pytest.mark.xdist_group` is already used elsewhere in this repo to pin related tests to one worker, which may be relevant \u2014 but whether the right fix is worker pinning, better fixture teardown, or something else is design work for grooming, not a stated requirement here.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Observed repeatedly while running the plugin suite during PR #3160 and #3165, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"0fbf5346d0591835a6cdec68fb88a7bcfd5018bf4014f32f4bf2f553d237932b\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQPWqqA\"}"}